what kind of gear can you use to record  videos for learning I'm a whir and I'm a  high school mathematics teacher years  ago I started recording my regular  classroom lessons to help out one of my  students who was sick and couldn't  attend school and much my surprised  people all around the world have found  these videos useful and watch them tens  of millions of times I've been asked by  lots of teachers how they themselves can  get started and using the power of  YouTube to help their students learn if  you're an accredited educator looking to  support your classes and share your  teaching with the world then this is for  you in this video I'm going to teach you  about the kind of gear that you can use  to make videos the way that I have and  there'll be two sections first I'll show  you how to record with the simplest  setup possible using your phone this is  how I started all those years ago  and cameras and smartphones have only  gotten better since then it's also a  camera that virtually everyone already  has and with a little extra equipment  for sound you can get some really good  quality footage out of it second for  those who are interested in a more  serious setup I'll show you the  equipment I currently use to record my  classroom lessons it's a little bit more  heavy-duty but it comes with some very  useful advantages which I'll unpack in  more detail later on so let's start with  your phone the first challenge when  you're filming with your phone is you  need to be able to position it somewhere  stable and most phones don't stand up  straight if you just set them on their  edge even those that do don't stay that  way for very long as I found out the  hard way thankfully there are lots of  little holders like this one which you  can use to fix your phone in a spot so  you can shoot let me show you how it  works is really simple you just kind of  take these draw parts here and then you  position your phone in there and it'll  clip into place and there you go this  particular one has a screw thread on the  bottom which you can attach to a stand  or a tripod like this once you've got in  place find any flat surface that's about  the right height if you've got a table  or I used to put a chair on top of a  table just be careful where you place  that and then once you've got that there  you're good to go  it'll stay in its place and you can  frame up just nicely other ones like  this come with a stand that's built-in  and whichever one you like just make  sure that you can  have a position so you can see  everything that you need like yourself a  whiteboard and whatever else you want in  the show and these are pretty useful but  don't feel that you need something this  fancy if you're just starting out when I  began I just knocked something together  in this school woodwork room that fit my  phone  you could even build a stair out of Lego  if you have some of that lying around so  you have something to keep your phone  steady what next there's some simple  gear that will help you improve the  quality of what you're recording for  your students now pretty much every  smartphone released in the last five  years will record in 4k resolution and  to give you a sense of what that means  if you're watching of your back  on a smartphone it's most likely being  shown to you in full HD or 1080p  resolution which is about 1/4 of the  pixels that your phone can actually  record when we try to say is you can get  a fairly sharp and detailed image out of  this camera the biggest single thing you  can do to improve the quality of your  videos is not about what you see it's  about what you hear sound is hugely  important and there are lots of options  let me show you some of the ones that I  have here this one is the rode videomic  me it plugs straight into the bottom of  your phone like this and it's what we  call a directional mic so wherever  you're pointing your camera it's going  to get audio from that same direction as  well  a very similar one here is the shore MV  88 same idea plugs into the bottom of  your phone it's very compact which is  very handy and has some additional  flexibility for which direction the  microphone is facing as well now both of  these are great if what you want is  something really tiny and small you can  put in your bag and not have to worry  about very much but if you're willing to  actually put a little effort in  something like this is even better and  this is a rode smartlav and lav is just  short for lavalier fancy name what that  means is the kind of microphone that you  can attach to your clothing I tend to  wear mine on my tie but you can put it  anywhere on your shirt so long as it's  nice and close to your mouth because of  that short distance you will always get  really great quality audio out of it  compared to something that is a few  meters away this end here is all of the  wires it's very very long which you can  see I can use to attach to the end of my  smartphone  just like a pair of headphones and now  whatever audio is directly from here  will record onto my phone and be part of  the same video file when I press record  super super handy also really quite  affordable to be honest so if you're  willing to sort of fuss around with  these wires and take them with you every  time this is going to be a really good  option for you and the Internet has  trained us all to get used to pretty  grainy video but sound quality is  something that makes a huge subconscious  difference especially when teaching  since so much of what we want to convey  is verbal and as an added bonus once  your audio quality improves you'll also  notice that the automatically generated  captions on YouTube will dramatically  increase in quality it won't be perfect  but there will definitely be good enough  to be useful which can make a huge  difference for our students with hearing  difficulties so this is your basic  starter kit phone stand and then your  microphone if you're just dipping your  toes into the water this is where I'd  encourage you to begin if you want to go  further though let me explain to you  what my current set-up is it has all the  same basic functions but I'll show you  the advantages that you gain from using  more advanced equipment a few years ago  I upgraded to this this is a Panasonic  gh4 if you want to know more specifics  about this camera itself there are  hundreds of reviews that you can find  online but I just want to explain the  particular benefits I get from using  this for recording my lessons first  thing is you can see it right on the  front a zoom lens now this is not a  special fancy one it's the one that came  with the camera and what this allows me  to do is have the camera at the back of  the classroom and the zoom lens means  that I can capture all of the action  right at the front they can see behind  this lens you've also got a sensor in  here which is far superior to the sensor  you'll get in your smartphone and what  that means is you can handle a wide  variety of lighting situations like  typical one when you've got a projector  on the screen in the background which is  very very bright and then you're in the  front which is quite dark so this camera  can manage both of those you also got  screen on the back here it flips out but  it's reversible so you can check the  shot while you're in it it's also a  Wi-Fi enabled camera so I can control  them remotely from my phone and one of  the best things about  this is that the batteries are  interchangeable so I can swap them in  and out in the middle of a lesson or  between lessons while the other battery  is on the charger and this is something  which caught me a lot of times when I  was recording during a normal day and  your phone battery will try its best to  soldier on but one of the most intense  things it does is recording videos so  it's going to get drained very fast and  having entertaining batteries is very  handy now when it records video it  records onto this SD card this is just a  kind of memory and why that's useful is  well you can have a whole bunch of these  which have much higher capacity than  what your phone does and let me just say  I've done it many times where I've been  recording and I've had a great lesson  that I've delivered but then my phone  has run out of memory in the middle  hasn't told me until the end and I don't  have the footage from it so this is an  advantage I will point out though that  if you go this path and you have these  little SD cards I'm some laptops some  computers you can't just find a card  reader that's built into the computer  plug it in and then you can get your  video files but often you can't do that  you'll need something separate so what  I've got here is a little card reader  card just goes in the top like that and  this will plug into your computer or  your iPad or whatever you use to edit  your videos now one last little feature  on here is also on the screen there's a  little readout here that you can see the  audio signal so you can know whether  you're recording sound properly or not  and let me just say I've done it many  times where I've forgotten to record  sound I just don't have something  plugged in so that will save you I  promise  now I've mentioned a lot of options for  microphones before but because I  normally record my lessons with my  camera all the way on the opposite side  of the room  now these options is really suitable  because they have wires that just kind  of go everywhere or they aren't long  enough so that's why these days I use  one of these it's a wireless lav mic it  attaches to my tie  then the transmitting radio sits in my  pocket and the receiving radio just sits  atop my camera and this particular kit  the rode wireless go can even be used  with the phone setup we were looking at  before so long as you get the correct  cables and adapters so in terms of  shredding videos that's all the gear  i've used throughout the time I've had  my youtube channel there's a simple set  up and the heavy duty gear but both of  them can enable you to create great  materials for your students in the next  video I'll show you how I use these  day-to-day in the classroom
 

